Looking at the problem, I need to determine whether an uncountable subset $G \subseteq \mathbb{R}$ exists such that for all $a, b, c, d \in G$, if $a - b = c - d$, then $a = c$ and $b = d$.

**Answer: No, such a set does not exist.** In fact, no such set with more than one element exists.

**Proof.**

Suppose, for the sake of contradiction, that $G \subseteq \mathbb{R}$ satisfies the stated property and contains at least two distinct elements $g_1, g_2 \in G$ with $g_1 \neq g_2$.

Set $a = g_1$, $b = g_1$, $c = g_2$, $d = g_2$. Then all four values lie in $G$, and we compute:
$$a - b = g_1 - g_1 = 0, \qquad c - d = g_2 - g_2 = 0.$$
Thus $a - b = c - d$. By the hypothesized property of $G$, it follows that $a = c$ and $b = d$. In particular, $a = c$ gives $g_1 = g_2$, contradicting our choice of $g_1 \neq g_2$.

Therefore $G$ can contain at most one element. Since any uncountable set is infinite (in particular has more than one element), no uncountable subset $G \subseteq \mathbb{R}$ satisfying the given condition can exist. $\blacksquare$

